WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.500
Hello everyone. Thank you for tuning in
to the langfocus channel. My name is Paul

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.660
Today's topic is the FSI free courses 
that are available online.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.920
So what is the FSI? That is 
the "Foreign Service Institute".

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:23.520
And that is where American diplomats or foreign 
service workers do their intensive language 
training before they are placed abroad.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.880
Now, why does that matter to us, to language learners?

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.060
Well, it's because the FSI materials, 
at least the older FSI materials,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.000
that they use for their intensive language study 
are available in the public domain.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.920
And you can get them on the internet for free.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.680
So that's very relevant to us. Anything that's free 
makes us pay a closer attention, doesn't it?

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.300
Well, you can also get them in hard copy format.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.220
You can get some repackaged versions like this.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.060
This one is called "Mastering Hebrew" 
but this is actually the FSI Hebrew course.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.060
Just some some entrepreneur 
repackage them and decided to sell it.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:03.240
I also have this one: "Mastering French" and
I have this one which is called just "Saudi Arabic".

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.040
That's the Saudi Arabic course from the FSI.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.160
So you can buy them in hard copy format
if you're willing to spend the money.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.160
If you want free one, if you want to try them, then 
they're public domain, you can find them on the internet.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.800
And I'll give you a link where you can find them later.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.660
So let's talk a little bit about the pros 
and cons of these FSI materials.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.140
First of all: the pros.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.240
The first thing is: they're free. 
So how can you go wrong with free materials.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.100
Even if they suck, then you stop using them 
and you have lost nothing. Right?

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.540
So you can download them, try them out, see how
it goes. They're free. What more could you ask for?

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.160
The second thing is that they are quite dense courses.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.580
There is quite a lot of material that you can use,
that you can get through.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.120
The Hebrew one is about five hundred and fifty pages.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.360
The French one well this is 
only one quarter of it right here in this.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.640
In this volume, this is 250 pages. 
Altogether, it's nine hundred or a thousand pages.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.320
And the Arabic one is 250 pages. 
So that's quite a lot of material for you to use.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.480
And there's hours and hours of audio to go with it. 
So it's just a lot that you can use.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.760
If you do work through that material, you'll gain
a good working knowledge of the language.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:11.840
That will probably also be very good for your sentence 
level of fluency because they just drilled the hell out of you.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.840
There are so many drills in these courses.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.540
There are question-and-answer drill,
substitution drills, transformation drills.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.060
Just drills drills drills. 
Not all the time, but a lot of the time.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.700
And it really helps you just spit out the sentences 
more quickly. It really helps improve your fluency.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.800
And to produce sentences on the go when you need to.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.980
So you don't have to spend as much
thinking time to produce your sentences.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:39.380
After finishing the FSI Hebrew course,
there was a huge jump in my fluency,
I could produce sentence as much more rapidly.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:43.460
I actually studied with the FSI course
between two trips to the Middle East.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.800
On the first trip, I couldn't really 
produce sentences very quickly.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.680
And on my second trip there, after using the FSI course, 
everybody told me "Wow, you speak like a native speaker".

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.580
I guess they're flattering me a little bit. But basically, doing so many drills helped me to speak fluently.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.700
And without a lot of pausing and hesitation.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:05.340
They're also good because the language presented 
is based on everyday common situation.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.760
Things like meeting someone for the first time,
ordering a ticket at the station, inviting someone out.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.520
That kind of thing. Talking about the weather.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:16.160
Those are the way they introduced the language
at the beginning of the lessons.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.020
And then, after that, they expand upon that,
by drilling the hell out of you but...

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.000
They introduced the language,
using those common situation.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.740
So everything you learn is quite useful and relevant 
and it's thematically based in each chapter.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.840
So, that helps you remember 
all of the new material you're learning.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.400
Now, for the cons or the bad.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.000
These courses are mind-numbingly boring. 
At least, some of them and at least at times.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.340
They can be really really drill-intensive and I remember
sitting there in the middle of these courses,

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.800
sometimes just counting down the minutes and okay, 
10 more minutes, 10 more minutes, I'm almost done.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.400
And just praying for the end 
because they really are boring.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.140
You need to be motivated yourself.
These courses will not motivate you.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:56.740
They're really dry a lot of the time 
and you just have to ...

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.560
push through them and that requires you to bring 
your own motivation to the table.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.700
Another downside is that the audio materials 
are just not that high quality.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.320
Because they were produced in the 1960s 
and 70s quite a long time ago.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.140
And the new CDs that you might get are
still based on those original master tapes.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.220
So sometimes they're not clear they're a bit fuzzy 
and they're just not really...

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.660
they're not really as good of a model as a digital audio 
recording would be, a modern recording. So...

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:29.820
So I would recommend that if you do use these FSI 
materials, you get some other more modern digitally
recorded audio materials,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.000
so that you can copy the pronunciation
of a native speaker clearly, right?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.400
Another downside is that these courses are not really 
made to be used as standalone materials,

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:44.500
they're made to be a manual for a classroom course
that is taught by an instructor.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:48.660
An instructor in these courses 
is supposed to give you extra activities,

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.640
that help you get more interactive experience 
and, sometimes, in some of the books,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.740
you can see notes to the instructor, like please do this, 
please prepare an activity for this purpose 
and that kind of thing.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:04.080
But, obviously, those activities are not in the book. 
They were up to the instructor of the classroom course.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.400
So this book don't come with a classroom course, 
you have to get that experience,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.320
that extra component that the teacher would be 
responsible for, you have to get that on your own.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.400
So really, you need to just get experience communicating
and interacting with native speakers.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.840
You need to take some lessons, you need to talk 
with friends to speak that language, 
just do that in addition to these courses.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:29.380
So, in conclusion, most people find these FSI courses 
really just too boring, too drill-intensive.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:34.240
But I do marginally recommend them for people 
who are just having trouble with fluency

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.920
and having trouble with sentence structure, people 
who take a long time to think about their sentences
and piece them together.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.920
If you want to gain that automaticity, that fluency, 
then you want to just practice with some drills.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.680
These are really good for drills. That's basically 
the main thing I recommend them for.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.340
If you want to just be able to spit out
sentences more quickly,

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.800
then, by all means, try these out, 
check out their drills.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.360
But bring your own motivation to the table 
because these courses are boring.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.180
And also just be prepared for some unclear audio.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:06.000
Don't expect 2015 level audio quality
because you won't get it.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.280
So supplement with some other audio
that you can model your pronunciation after.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.900
Thank you for tuning in to the langfocus channel.
Have a good night. Talk to you later.

